        "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Sodium is the most abundant cation in the extracellular fluid. Hyponatremia usually reflects hypo-osmolality and is typically associated with a decreased total body sodium content. Either water retention or solute loss can cause hyponatremia. Most solute loss occurs in iso-osmotic solutions (e.g., vomit and diarrhea) and, as a result, water retention in relation to solute is the underlying cause in almost all patients with hyponatremia. In general, hyponatremia occurs only when a defect in renal water excretion is present.",
              "pdf_page": 816,
              "printed_page": 734,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SYSTEMS AFFECTED": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "canonical_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Nervous—severe neurologic dysfunction is not usually seen until serum sodium concen tration falls below 110–115 mEq/L. Clinical signs may be more related to the rate of decline in serum sodium concentration than the actual nadir. Dogs with chronic hyponatremia often have mild, if any, clinical signs.",
              "pdf_page": 816,
              "printed_page": 734,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Overly rapid correction of hyponatremia can also cause neurologic damage.",
              "pdf_page": 816,
              "printed_page": 734,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
